body:
Format migration: the second half of `kalide upgrade` — carrying a project's structural/format version forward across kalide versions through an ordered, versioned, kalide-defined migration registry. The anchors are a template library's required `library.yaml` `format:` integer (domain.value.library-manifest; only `1` has ever been accepted, so today `1` is the only format in existence — internal/template.loadLibraryMeta rejects any other value) and the format the running binary implements (domain.value.version, the build-stamped `main.version` printed by version-command). A migration is a defined, ordered, versioned transformation from one source format to the next; adding one is a kalide change, and the kinds it may take are examples only — a future `library.yaml` `format:` bump with the schema rewrites it governs, a future `template.yaml` schema change, or a future deck-config change (domain.value.format-migration) — none of which exists today. Upgrade applies the registered migrations from the project's current format to the highest format the running binary implements, in order, each at most once, preserving author values (a library's `name`/`description`, a deck's title/author/date/properties) and changing only the format/structure the migration governs.

The registered migration set is empty today. There is no format older than `1`: `loadLibraryMeta` has only ever accepted `1`, so no library can be at an older format, and a deck's `kalide.yaml` carries no version field at all (its key set is title, author, date, theme, navigation, properties, templates — internal/deck/config.go). Consequently there is no migration to apply and none is invented from a project's content: upgrading a current (`format: 1`) library, and upgrading any deck, is a no-op on the migration half — the files are left byte-for-byte unchanged and the command exits 0. The framework exists so that adding a migration is a kalide change: a future format bump ships its ordered migration step in the registry, applied to a project at the matching older format. Adding a migration is never inferred from a project's files.

A format the binary does not recognize is reported, never guessed or silently migrated. "Newer than the running kalide" is defined on the format integer, not on any version string: the running binary's format is the highest `library.yaml` format it implements (today `1`), and a `dev`/unstamped build implements the same format as the source it was built from, so `dev` never means "newer". A library whose `format:` is unknown or greater than the highest format the binary implements is left byte-for-byte untouched and the command exits non-zero naming the format found and the format supported (global.constraint.upgrade-unknown-format-reported). A gap in the migration chain counts as unknown too: a format older than `1` that no registered migration chain reaches — because the chain to the running binary's format is missing a step — is refused the same way, byte-for-byte untouched and non-zero, never treated as migratable (checkSupportedLibraryFormat). The loader must be able to read every format the migrator reads: internal/template.loadLibraryMeta today accepts only `1`, so a future registered migration could not read its source format until the loader accepts/recognizes that older format — the loader is required to accept every older format the migrator reads; today only `1` exists, so this capability is required but inert. A deck, which has no version field, is never assumed to be at any version: no deck format migration is registered today, so the deck migration path is a no-op and nothing is inferred from the deck's content (requirements.note.upgrade-deck-vs-library) — a deck's kalide-owned files are recognized by content only in the refresh half, never by an invented version. The result of a migration is validated before it is kept (a library re-loads through the template library loader; a deck through the deck loader); a multi-file migration is all-or-nothing, so a failure leaves the files it would have written as they were (requirements.constraint.upgrade-migrations-ordered-atomic, global.constraint.upgrade-never-breaks-project).

AC:
- a library at `format: 1` (the only format that exists) has no migration applied: the command exits 0 and every file is byte-for-byte unchanged;
- a library whose `format:` is unknown (e.g. `0`, or an older format no registered migration chain reaches) or greater than the highest format the binary implements (e.g. `2`) fails non-zero, names the format found and the format supported, and is left byte-for-byte untouched;
- no migration is invented from content: with the registry empty, no deck or library structural rewrite happens today (a current project is a no-op; an unrecognized format is refused, never rewritten);
- a `dev`/unstamped build compares on the format integer, so it never treats a project as newer because the version string is `dev`, and it never refuses a supported format;
- internal/template.loadLibraryMeta accepts every format the migrator reads — today that is exactly `{1}`: a `format: 1` library loads and a `format: 2` library is refused;
- a future migration (none today) would be validated before it is kept; a migration that cannot complete leaves every file it would have written byte-for-byte unchanged (no partial migration);
- the upgraded project validates: `kalide start` succeeds for a deck and the library loads cleanly (templates-dir-validation).
